{
  "question": "Why do both pendulum instruments (lab and obs) show a statistically significant downward drift in measured period, while the resonator instruments show no comparable drift, despite being operated in the same settlement environment?",
  "hypotheses": [
    {
      "label": "Temperature‑induced change in pendulum length (thermal expansion/contraction)",
      "probability": 0.35,
      "rationale": "A modest ambient temperature shift could alter the effective length of the pendulums, shortening their period. Resonators are less sensitive to small length changes, so they would not exhibit a similar drift. The observed downward drift in both pendulums and the absence of drift in resonators matches this pattern.",
      "evidenceFor": ["883723","20625","606067","198503","124","125","126","127"],
      "evidenceAgainst": ["413807","538937","590","496956","19308","396359"]
    },
    {
      "label": "Shared timing‑circuit drift (electronics aging or clock speed change)",
      "probability": 0.30,
      "rationale": "Both pendulum setups use the same timing module, which may have experienced a gradual frequency increase, causing measured periods to appear shorter. Resonators use a different timing chain, explaining their stability.",
      "evidenceFor": ["883723","20625","606067","198503","124","125","126","127"],
      "evidenceAgainst": ["413807","538937","590","496956","19308","396359"]
    },
    {
      "label": "Mechanical wear or gradual shortening of pendulum suspensions",
      "probability": 0.20,
      "rationale": "Slow changes in suspension length or elasticity could reduce the pendulum period. This would affect both pendulums but not resonators. However, identical magnitude of drift in two separate pendulums is less common, lowering confidence.",
      "evidenceFor": ["883723","20625","606067","198503","124","125","126","127"],
      "evidenceAgainst": ["413807","538937","590","496956","19308","396359"]
    },
    {
      "label": "Software or data‑processing error applied only to pendulum recordings",
      "probability": 0.10,
      "rationale": "A bug in the pendulum data‑handling pipeline (e.g., an incorrect calibration factor) could bias all pendulum results low while leaving resonator data untouched. The systematic nature of the drift across many trials supports this, but no direct evidence of a code change has been observed.",
      "evidenceFor": ["883723","20625","606067","198503","124","125","126","127"],
      "evidenceAgainst": ["413807","538937","590","496956","19308","396359"]
    },
    {
      "label": "Residual (unconsidered factor)",
      "probability": 0.05,
      "rationale": "Any other unknown influence not captured by the above categories.",
      "evidenceFor": [],
      "evidenceAgainst": []
    }
  ],
  "residual": 0.05,
  "summaryOfChange": "The previous question focused narrowly on pendulum_obs drift. New data reveals parallel downward drifts in both pendulum_lab and pendulum_obs, while resonator instruments remain stable, indicating a broader, shared effect on pendulums. Accordingly, the hypothesis set has been rewritten to address this larger anomaly, reallocating probabilities based on the strength of the observed drifts (evidence IDs for pendulum measurements) and the lack of comparable drift in resonator data (evidence IDs for resonator measurements). Mundane explanations (temperature, timing‑circuit drift, mechanical wear, software error) now dominate, reflecting the higher prior plausibility of such factors, with a small residual left for unknown causes."
}

