    {
      "label": "Gradual mechanical wear or shortening of the pendulum suspensions",
      "probability": 0.45,
      "rationale": "Both pendulums show statistically significant downward drift (z = –2.01 for lab, –1.19 for obs) yet their reading‑by‑reading agreement is at chance level (0.293 vs chance 0.296). Independent wear of each suspension could produce similar directional drift without enforcing strong correlation, matching the data.",
      "evidenceFor": ["490408","449521","358351","315951","963569","473733","820425","652794","558132","814336","553001","3929","757206"],
      "evidenceAgainst": []
    },
    {
      "label": "Gradual increase in the frequency of the shared timing circuit used by the pendulum setups",
      "probability": 0.20,
      "rationale": "A common timing module would affect both pendulums, explaining the concurrent drift. However, the low cross‑instrument correlation (≈ chance) slightly weakens this hypothesis, as a shared electronic drift would be expected to produce more correlated fluctuations.",
      "evidenceFor": ["490408","449521","358351","315951","963569","473733","820425","652794","558132","814336","553001","3929","757206"],
      "evidenceAgainst": ["crosspend_lab_obs"]
    },
    {
      "label": "Environmental temperature change affecting pendulum length",
      "probability": 0.15,
      "rationale": "A modest rise in ambient temperature could contract the pendulum support, shortening the period. This would affect both pendulums but not resonators. The hypothesis is downgraded because no temperature recordings are available and the low inter‑instrument correlation is inconsistent with a single ambient driver.",
      "evidenceFor": ["490408","449521","358351","315951","963569","473733","820425","652794","558132","814336","553001","3929","757206"],
      "evidenceAgainst": ["crosspend_lab_obs"]
    },
    {
      "label": "Software or data‑processing error applied only to pendulum recordings",
      "probability": 0.10,
      "rationale": "A bug in the pendulum data pipeline could introduce a systematic bias toward shorter periods. The lack of an obvious code change and the low correlation between the two pendulum data streams reduce confidence in this explanation.",
      "evidenceFor": ["490408","449521","358351","315951","963569","473733","820425","652794","558132","814336","553001","3929","757206"],
      "evidenceAgainst": ["crosspend_lab_obs"]
    },
    {
      "label": "Residual (unconsidered) factor",
      "probability": 0.10,
      "rationale": "There remains a possibility of an unknown influence (e.g., subtle magnetic field variation, power supply ripple) that has not yet been identified.",
      "evidenceFor": [],
      "evidenceAgainst": []
    }
  ],
  "residual": 0.10,
  "summaryOfChange": "The question was reformulated to focus explicitly on the divergent drift between pendulums and resonators. Probabilities have been updated: mechanical wear is now the leading hypothesis (up from 0.19 to 0.45) because the drift exists and the low cross‑instrument correlation supports independent physical changes. Shared timing drift and temperature hypotheses have been reduced (from 0.29/0.33 to 0.20/0.15) due to the same correlation evidence, while the software error hypothesis is unchanged but remains low. A residual category is retained for any unknown factors. All probability entries sum to 1.0."
}

